            <ArticleTitle>Isolation of Immune Cells for Adoptive Transfer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>321-344</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/978-1-4939-6625-7_25</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Adoptive transfer of T lymphocytes is a useful technique to characterize the role of the immune system in hypertension and vascular disease. Here we describe as an example the isolation of splenic T regulatory cells from donor mice processed to obtain a single cell suspension, followed by negative and positive selection to obtain CD4+ T cells and CD4+CD25+ Treg cells, respectively. Treg cells can be subsequently transferred to recipient animals.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
